<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as LDS Church, Mormon Church</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a> of Latter-day Saints is the largest church of the Latter Day Saint movement, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-smith">Joseph Smith</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1830. Persecuted in Missouri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, where Smith was killed in 1844, its members followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigham-young">Brigham Young</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-lake-valley">Salt Lake Valley</a> in 1847 and colonised much of the Great Basin. Headquartered in Salt Lake City, it has grown into a worldwide church with millions of members.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-of-jesus-christ-of-latter-day-saints.jpg" alt="Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints" width="330" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diliff · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salt_Lake_Temple,_Utah_-_Sept_2004-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The church arose during the Second Great Awakening. Joseph Smith formally organised it as the Church of Christ on 6 April 1830 in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Early converts were drawn in part by the newly published <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-mormon">Book of Mormon</a>, a self-described chronicle of Indigenous American prophets that Smith said he had translated from golden plates. After Smith's death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigham-young">Brigham Young</a> and the largest body of his followers incorporated the church in 1851 under the name The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints. Members hold that after the death of Jesus and his apostles the authority of the original church was lost, and that it was restored through Smith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under Smith the church's headquarters moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, Missouri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>. In Missouri fear of its political and military power led to the Mormon War of 1838 and Governor Lilburn Boggs's Mormon Extermination Order. In 1844 the Nauvoo Expositor criticised plural marriage and other doctrines. Smith took a leading part in destroying its press, was charged with treason and jailed, and was shot dead by a mob that stormed the jail. After the succession crisis most followers sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigham-young">Brigham Young</a>, who led them to Nebraska and in 1847 to what later became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah-territory">Utah Territory</a>. About 80,000 settlers arrived between 1847 and 1869. Plural marriage, announced publicly in 1852, was curtailed in 1890 and officially rescinded in 1904.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The church is led by a president, regarded by members as a prophet, seer and revelator, over a hierarchy of areas, stakes and wards led by volunteer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. Its canon consists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-mormon">Book of Mormon</a>, the Doctrine and Covenants and the Pearl of Great Price. Members believe in Jesus Christ as the Son of God but reject the ecumenical creeds and the Trinity, holding that God the Father, Jesus and the Holy Ghost are three distinct beings united in purpose. Members observe the Word of Wisdom, which forbids alcohol, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and tobacco, and give a tenth of their income. Young men are expected to serve a two-year mission.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Word of Wisdom, a health code of the <b>Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints</b> that some scholars think was influenced by the movement, was the basis of a call in 1921 from Heber J. Grant for the Latter-day Saints to adhere strictly to it, including complete…</blockquote><a href="/movement/temperance-movement">Temperance movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It has also been admired by Orthodox Christian thinkers and by members of the <b>Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints</b>, and in recent times Kevin Anthony set it as a choral work, performed by the BYU Singers, and David Bentley Hart drew on it for his book…</blockquote><a href="/songs/hymn-of-the-pearl">Hymn of the Pearl</a></li><li><blockquote>Karen Lynn Davidson's version, which draws on parts of 2 Nephi in the Book of Mormon, is sung in the hymnal of the <b>Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-sacred-head-now-wounded">O Sacred Head, Now Wounded</a></li><li><blockquote>Driven from Illinois, members of the <b>Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints</b> reached the Salt Lake Valley in 1847, then in Mexican territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-deseret">State of Deseret</a></li></ul></section>
